    "reasoning": [
      {
        "kind": "heading",
        "text": "ACTC total claims, TY2027 threshold-one-dollar conditional"
      },
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "text": "Target is the registered ledger target: IRS SOI Publication 1304 Table 3.3, all returns total row, refundable child tax credit or additional child tax credit, number of returns, tax year 2027 first print. The ledger uses 2029-12-31 as the resolution date because IRS SOI gives a release window for this future publication rather than a daily 2029 calendar date available this run. Publication 4801 is supporting pre-resolution evidence only; Publication 1304 Table 3.3 remains the sole resolver."
      },
      {
        "kind": "tool",
        "tool": "irs.pub4801.lookup",
        "call": "Open IRS Publication 4801 Rev. 6-2026, TY2023 line item estimates; inspect Schedule 8812 totals and line 27.",
        "result": "IRS Publication 4801 TY2023: all individual returns filed 160,602,107; Schedule 8812 total filed 47,815,267; Schedule 8812 line 16a returns 19,519,324; line 17 returns 17,777,289; line 27 additional child tax credit returns 17,401,584."
      },
      {
        "kind": "tool",
        "tool": "irs.pub4801.lookup",
        "call": "Open IRS Publication 4801 Rev. 12-2024, TY2022 line item estimates; inspect Schedule 8812 line 27.",
        "result": "IRS Publication 4801 TY2022: all individual returns filed 161,336,659; Schedule 8812 total filed 47,403,924; Schedule 8812 line 16a returns 19,214,388; line 17 returns 17,632,514; line 27 additional child tax credit returns 17,219,508."
      },
      {
        "kind": "tool",
        "tool": "law.lookup",
        "call": "Inspect US Code/congressional text for IRC 24(d)(1)(B)(i) threshold mechanics and proposed threshold-one-dollar legislation.",
        "result": "Current-law materials identify the ACTC earned-income formula in IRC 24(d)(1)(B)(i); Congress bill text S.3596 says section 24(d)(1)(B)(i) would be amended by striking '$3,000' and inserting '$1'. IRS 2026 revenue procedure states the refundable portion for taxable years beginning in 2026 is $1,700."
      },
      {
        "kind": "tool",
        "tool": "irs.release.lookup",
        "call": "Inspect IRS SOI publication and upcoming-release pages for resolver and release policy.",
        "result": "IRS Publication 1304 page says the Individual Complete Report contains complete individual income tax data, all tables are available as Excel files, and Table 3.3 is All Returns: Tax Liability, Tax Credits, and Tax Payments. IRS SOI upcoming releases page says release dates are tentative and schedules are updated; the canonical ledger target supplies expected 2029 release window ending 2029-12-31."
      },
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "text": "Base rate/reference class: recent actual ACTC return counts are 17.219508M in TY2022 and 17.401584M in TY2023, a +0.182076M one-year move. The more relevant policy pool is TY2023 Schedule 8812 line 16a, 19.519324M returns with child credit above the nonrefundable amount before the earned-income formula, versus 17.401584M final ACTC returns, leaving a 2.117740M near-term pool plausibly blocked by the earned-income formula. I treat most of this gap as convertible to additional claim returns under a $1 threshold because the policy changes eligibility for returns that already have calculated refundable potential, while recognizing that some of the gap becomes larger refunds for existing filers or remains blocked by non-threshold rules."
      },
      {
        "kind": "math",
        "text": "Model candidates: persistence candidate = TY2023 actual line 27 = 17.4M, 80% fallback interval from recent observed ACTC level/potential-pool spread = 17.2M to 19.5M. Policy-mechanism candidate = TY2023 potential pool 19.519324M + 0.3M demographic/filing drift to TY2027 + 0.3M higher refundable cap/CTC-induced additional low-tax-liability filers = 20.119324M, rounded forecast point 20.1M. Override persistence because the conditioning event directly changes the earned-income threshold that blocks some otherwise eligible ACTC returns."
      },
      {
        "kind": "math",
        "text": "Interval: realized recent level spread from actual ACTC line 27 to potential line 16a is 2.117740M, and 2022-to-2023 actual movement is 0.182076M. I use an asymmetric 80% policy interval of point -1.7M / +2.3M: lower side allows many line-16a nonclaimants to have zero/no qualifying earned income, already-claiming returns receiving higher amounts rather than increasing return counts, or failures of SSN/filing requirements; upper side allows outreach, filing, and higher 2027 credit parameters to pull in returns beyond the 2023 line-16a pool. Rounded forecast bounds: 18.4M to 22.4M."
      },
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "text": "Prior/update/interval: prior is TY2023 persistence at 17.4M ACTC returns; historical sample is TY2022-TY2023 Schedule 8812 line 27 plus TY2023 Schedule 8812 line 16a; adjustment components are +2.1M from removing the earned-income threshold block, +0.3M filing/demographic drift, +0.3M higher-credit/participation effect; interval method is realized policy-pool spread widened for 2027 law and take-up uncertainty, implying 18.4M to 22.4M."
      },
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "text": "Counter-consideration: outcome lands below 18.4M if most low-earnings line-16a returns have no positive earned income, the enacted law adds offsetting SSN or work-eligible identification restrictions, the threshold cut mostly raises amounts for already-claiming returns instead of return counts, or IRS reporting classifies some claims outside the Table 3.3 ACTC line. It lands above 22.4M if the enacted package also makes the credit more fully refundable, expands qualifying children, funds major nonfiler outreach, or materially raises the refundable cap beyond the current indexed path."
      },
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "text": "Review disposition: accepted the resolver fix by removing resolver-side rounding from the observation and accepted the evidentiary-bridge critique by distinguishing conversion of the 2.117740M line-16a gap into additional return counts from larger amounts for already-claiming returns. Also clarified that Publication 4801 is supporting evidence only and kept Publication 1304 Table 3.3 as the resolver."
      },
